WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
What am I most excited for in the next release of Positron?

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.100
I would say number one is the import data functionality.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.100
Functionality.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.500
Number one is the import data functionality.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.500
So this is something that was highly requested by users, where

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.299
you can click on like a CSV or a parquet file.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.100
You can open it up, play around with it in the data explorer,

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.500
add your filters, do whatever you want to it.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.799
And then with the click of a button, it will turn it into a

00:00:23.799 --> 00:00:28.400
full Python script, including your filters and import it from

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.799
like the file path itself.

00:00:30.299 --> 00:00:33.099
In Positron, I'm really excited about the ways that you can

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.400
install Python.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.099
It has been a joke in the Python system for a very long time,

00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:39.900
but it's so hard to install.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.400
Nowadays, I can delete all of the Pythons off of my laptop and

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.400
go to the select interpreter button inside of Positron and

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.400
it automatically comes up with a button to install Python.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.500
I just choose whatever version I want and using uv, it will

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.599
set everything up for me and I don't have to sweat over installing

00:00:58.599 --> 00:01:00.099
Python on a new machine anymore.

00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:05.000
A lot of people have been asking about inline output for Cordo

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:07.500
and now it is available in Positron.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.000
Tell your friends, tell your enemies, inline output is in

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:11.900
Positron.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.800
So if you've been waiting to try Positron because inline output

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.000
is not available, now is the time to download it.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.500
One of my favorite things about working in open source is the

00:01:21.599 --> 00:01:23.500
really cool people in our community.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.400
And recently folks in the Julia community have made an extension.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.300
So now you can install Julia for Python on OpenVSX and Julia

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.500
works inside Positron.

00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:35.000
You can see all your variables.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.300
You can use the console.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.000
And so now Positron is a fantastic place to be running Julia

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.400
code. We love to be involved in whatever projects or whatever

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.699
things you're seeing inside of Positron.

00:01:45.699 --> 00:01:49.400
So if you ever see anything and you wish it existed or maybe

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.800
you wish it didn't exist, we're excited to hear about any feedback

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.000
you have for Positron. Just like me on day two of conferences,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.699
I'm just doing strange side quests.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:01:59.800
That's my makeup now.

